<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>How the GLB mesh detection works</strong> — meshes are auto-classified by name keywords. Any mesh with "body", "hood", "door", "roof" etc. gets primary paint. "rim", "spoke", "alloy" get rim color. "glass", "window" get transparent glass material. If your model uses generic names like <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">Mesh_004</code>, click the <strong>MESH MAP</strong> button in the UI — it lists every mesh with its detected slot so you can build your <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">meshOverrides</code> object.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>PBR material system by paint type:</strong></p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto w-full px-2 mb-6"><table class="min-w-full border-collapse text-sm leading-[1.7] whitespace-normal"><thead class="text-left"><tr><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-border-300/60 py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Type</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-border-300/60 py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Roughness</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-border-300/60 py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Metalness</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-border-300/60 py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Clearcoat</th><th scope="col" class="text-text-100 border-b-0.5 border-border-300/60 py-2 pr-4 align-top font-bold">Special</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Metallic</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.22</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.85</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.8</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">—</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Matte</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.94</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.05</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">—</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Chrome</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.0</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">1.0</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">1.0</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Mirror</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Metals (Brushed)</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.4</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.92</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.3</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">—</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">Chameleon</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.15</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">0.8</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top">1.0</td><td class="border-b-0.5 border-border-300/30 py-2 pr-4 align-top"><strong>Iridescence ON</strong> — this is the key one, genuinely looks like paint shift</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">The Chameleon paints use Three.js <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">MeshPhysicalMaterial</code> iridescence which is a real thin-film interference simulation — the color actually shifts as the camera angle changes, just like GTA's shift paints.</p></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-2 py-2"><div class="flex text-left font-ui rounded-lg overflow-hidden border-0.5 transition duration-300 w-full hover:bg-bg-000/50 px-4 border-border-300/15 hover:border-border-200" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Open artifact: Carbuilderglb"><div class="artifact-block-cell group/artifact-block flex flex-1 align-start justify-between w-full"><div class="flex flex-1 gap-2 min-w-0"><div class="flex items-end w-[68px] relative shrink-0"><div class="absolute right-2 flex flex-1 overflow-hidden w-[52px] h-[71px] rounded-t-lg border-0.5 border-border-200 select-none scale-[1] group-hover/artifact-block:scale-[1.035] -rotate-[0.1rad] group-hover/artifact-block:-rotate-[0.065rad] duration-300 ease-out group-hover/artifact-block:duration-400 group-hover/artifact-block:ease-[cubic-bezier(0,0.9,0.5,1.35)] transition-transform backface-hidden will-change-transform translate-y-[19%] bg-gradient-to-b from-bg-000 to-bg-000/0 pt-4 items-start justify-center h-full"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 256 256" class="text-text-500"><path d="M67.84,92.61,25.37,128l42.47,35.39a6,6,0,1,1-7.68,9.22l-48-40a6,6,0,0,1,0-9.22l48-40a6,6,0,0,1,7.68,9.22Zm176,30.78-48-40a6,6,0,1,0-7.68,9.22L230.63,128l-42.47,35.39a6,6,0,1,0,7.68,9.22l48-40a6,6,0,0,0,0-9.22Zm-81.79-89A6,6,0,0,0,154.36,38l-64,176A6,6,0,0,0,94,221.64a6.15,6.15,0,0,0,2,.36,6,6,0,0,0,5.64-3.95l64-176A6,6,0,0,0,162.05,34.36Z"></path></svg></div></div><div class="flex flex-col gap-1 py-4 min-w-0 f