transparent [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8"><div><div class="standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Good points on both! For the wheels — since this is a static GLB of the Mustang, the wheel meshes are already part of the model (Object_25, 40, 49, 51). The rim color is already being applied to those. The wheel <strong>images</strong> from your JSON are just 2D reference photos from the GTA Wiki — they can't be projected onto the 3D model geometry.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">The cleanest solution is to <strong>replace the GLB's rim meshes at runtime</strong> with procedurally generated 3D wheel geometry that matches the selected wheel style. We can build spoke patterns that approximate the Benny's/Bespoke designs.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex justify-start opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:opacity-100 transition" role="group" aria-label="Message actions"><div class="text-text-300"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><div class="w-fit" data-state="closed"><button class="inline-flex
