transparent [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8"><div><div class="standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Glad it's all working! Honestly the current <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">21/9</code> aspect ratio with the wheel panel eating into the width means the car is a bit cramped. Here are my thoughts and some questions to make sure we get it right:</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>On size</strong> — yes the car should be bigger. The wheel panel beside it is taking ~112px from an already wide-but-short canvas. Moving the wheel info somewhere else would give the car a lot more breathing room.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>On multiple vehicles</strong> — totally doable. GTA Online has 8 vehicle classes worth showing: Sports, Muscle, Super, SUV, Motorcycle, Coupe, Sedan, Off-road. Each would need its own GLB in <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">/public/models/</code>.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex justify-start opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:opacity-100 transition" role="group" aria-label="Message actions"><div class="text-text-300"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><div class="w-fit" data-state="closed"><button class="inline-flex
