transparent [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.standard-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pl-2 [&amp;_.progressive-markdown_:is(p,blockquote,ul,ol,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6)]:pr-8"><div><div class="standard-markdown grid-cols-1 grid [&amp;_&gt;_*]:min-w-0 gap-3 standard-markdown"><p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">Short answer: <strong>no, stick with GLB.</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">RPF is Rockstar's proprietary archive format — it's essentially a container that holds multiple assets (models, textures, scripts, collision data) bundled together in an encrypted/compressed format specific to RAGE engine. You can't load it directly in Three.js or any web renderer.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">The conversion pipeline would be:
<strong>RPF → OpenIV/CodeWalker extract → YDR/YDD (Rockstar model format) → Blender with a GTA V import plugin → GLB export</strong></p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">That's 3-4 tools, manual work per vehicle, and the output is still a GLB at the end. You're already at the finish line with the current approach.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>The direct Sketchfab route is better because:</strong></p>
<ul class="[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3">
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Someone has already done the extraction and conversion work</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Game-ripped models from GTA V specifically are already optimized for real-time rendering</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Low poly counts, clean UVs, no collision mesh bloat</li>
<li class="whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">Many are already exported as GLB/GLTF ready to drop in</li>
</ul>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]"><strong>The one case where RPF makes sense</strong> is if you want a very specific vehicle that isn't on Sketchfab — like a modded or rare GTA Online car. In that case the pipeline above works but it's about 30-45 minutes per vehicle even if you know what you're doing.</p>
<p class="font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]">For the 6 remaining vehicles, searching Sketchfab for <code class="bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">[real world equivalent] low poly</code> or checking gta5-mods.com for already-exported OBJ/FBX files (which Blender can convert to GLB in one click) is the fastest path.</p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex justify-start opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 group-focus-within:opacity-100 transition" role="group" aria-label="Message actions"><div class="text-text-300"><div class="text-text-300 flex items-stretch justify-between"><div class="w-fit" data-state="closed"><button class="inline-flex
